   589	//
   590	// The paced pair above mirrors TC-57's shape. It is not, on its own, enough to
   591	// support a NEGATIVE: "0/10 at this load" says nothing about a wider window. The
   592	// stress pair widens every knob that could plausibly open one:
   593	//
   594	//   * `burst: 24` > `PROJECTION_COMMIT_BATCH` (16), so the worker commits FULL
   595	//     16-job transactions — the longest write-lock hold it ever takes, i.e. the
   596	//     widest target the promotion can be offered.
   597	//   * `writer_threads: 2` == `PROJECTION_WORKERS`, so both workers can be
   598	//     committing while the transition loop runs.
   599	//   * `writer_pace_ms: 1`, near-saturating: the competing threads contend for the
   600	//     connection mutex almost continuously, which maximises the chance one of them
   601	//     wins the mutex in the gap between `transition`'s `drain` returning and its
   602	//     own acquisition. MEASURED: at `writer_pace_ms: 0` the load is so heavy that
   603	//     `transition`'s own `drain` NEVER reaches idle and every call burns the full
   604	//     30 s timeout into `EngineError::Scheduler` (5 attempts in 180 s, 4 of them
   605	//     `Scheduler`) — an arm that cannot measure the promote race at all.
   606	//   * `embed_delay_ms: 0`: the worker commits microseconds after the competing
   607	//     write releases the connection mutex, rather than 1-2 ms after. This is the
   608	//     knob that MATTERS — see [`ArmConfig::embed_delay_ms`].
   609	//
   610	// A `Scheduler` error here is NOT the defect under test — it is `transition`'s
   611	// own `drain` burning its 30 s timeout under saturating load. It is counted and
   612	// reported SEPARATELY for exactly that reason, and it is a finding in its own
   613	// right (see the design doc).
