     1	//! 0.8.20 Slice 23 (R-20-SV, leg 2) — **TC-91 characterization harness.**
     2	//!
     3	//! TC-91 (ledger `seq-129`, p2) is two defects that share one mechanism:
     4	//!
     5	//! * **(a)** roughly half of all embeds at baseline are DUPLICATES. It reproduces
     6	//!   on the ANONYMOUS arm too, so it is not the governed-write race TC-57 fixed,
     7	//!   and it did not go away with that fix. Harm class: wasted embed work.
     8	//!   Invisible to correctness tests, because a duplicate embed produces the right
     9	//!   answer — twice.
    10	//! * **(b)** the projection worker's commit is invoked as
    11	//!   `let _ = commit_projection_outcomes(..)` (`lib.rs:12711` and `lib.rs:12824`).
    12	//!   A commit failure is dropped with no error, no log and no telemetry.
    13	//!
    14	//! **CHARACTERIZATION ONLY — NO FIX** (steward `seq-136`: the fix lands at
    15	//! 0.8.21). `src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs` is byte-identical to the
    16	//! baseline `94f09d7d` in the commit that lands this file. Written
    17	//! characterization: `dev/design/0.8.20-tc90-tc91-characterization.md`.
    18	//!
    19	//! ## The durable point of (b), and why it is the most valuable thing here
    20	//!
    21	//! ```text
    22	//! lib.rs:12711   let _ = commit_projection_outcomes(connection, &outcomes, shared);
    23	//! lib.rs:12824   let _ = commit_projection_outcomes(connection, &outcomes, shared);
    24	//! ```
    25	//!
    26	//! A `'failed'` terminal and a `projection_failures` audit row are written by the
    27	//! `ProjectionOutcome::Failure` arm (`lib.rs:13962-13984`), which represents an
    28	//! **embed** failure. A **commit** failure never reaches that arm at all: the
    29	//! whole transaction rolls back, the row keeps `terminal IS NULL`, the worker loop
    30	//! sets `pending_scan = true` (`lib.rs:12663`), the dispatcher re-fetches the row
    31	//! and it is embedded AGAIN.
    32	//!
    33	//! Therefore **counting `'failed'` terminal states is STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE of
    34	//! seeing this class of failure.** Any future attempt to quantify worker
    35	//! reliability that way will report a falsely clean number — zero — no matter how
    36	//! many commits are being lost. [`tc91_forced_commit_failure_is_invisible_to_terminal_counting`]
    37	//! proves this by forcing the failure and measuring both.
    38	//!
    39	//! ## The measurement trap that had to be disarmed FIRST
    40	//!
    41	//! `run_vector_equivalence_probe` (`lib.rs:15309`) embeds the 45-probe fixture at
    42	//! OPEN. Two different figures are on the record and BOTH are true, of different
    43	//! opens: **90** embeds on the population open (populate 45 + check 45), **45** on
    44	//! every open thereafter. Ledger `seq-125` (the TC-71 close) states the
    45	//! consequence: *absolute embed counts are unusable as an oracle across a reopen*
    46	//! — every embed assertion must be a DELTA from a post-open snapshot.
    47	//!
    48	//! **And TC-68 LANDED in Slice 22 (`572475f2`)**, adding the fingerprint verdict
    49	//! cache (`probe_verification_is_cached`, `lib.rs:15566`). The ledger note calling
    50	//! the 45 re-embeds something TC-68 "is scheduled to cache away" PREDATES that
    51	//! landing, so the 45 must not be inherited as a constant.
    52	//! [`tc91_probe_embed_cost_per_open_at_head`] MEASURES it at HEAD instead.
    53	//!
    54	//! Every duplicate figure below is therefore taken from a SINGLE open whose
    55	//! embed log starts empty, and ingest embeds are separated from probe embeds by
    56	//! text: this fixture's bodies all carry [`INGEST_MARKER`], which no probe text
    57	//! contains. The two counts are reported separately, never summed.
    58	//!
    59	//! **MEASURED at HEAD (`94f09d7d`)** by [`tc91_probe_embed_cost_per_open_at_head`]:
    60	//! open 1 (no vector kind) **0**; open 2 (populate + check) **90**; open 3, after
    61	//! TC-68's verdict cache, **0**. The "45 on every open thereafter" figure is
    62	//! therefore NO LONGER TRUE at HEAD — TC-68 took the steady-state reopen probe
    63	//! cost to zero, not merely reduced it. `probe_calls == 0` is asserted in every
    64	//! duplicate arm, so the separation is verified rather than assumed.
    65	//!
    66	//! ## MEASURED at `94f09d7d` — and (a) turns out to BE (b)
    67	//!
    68	//! | arm | shape | runs | ingest duplicates / rows |
    69	//! |---|---|---|---|
    70	//! | [`tc91_duplicate_embed_rate_governed`] | 200 x 1-row writes, 1 ms apart | 10 | 102-106 / 200 (**52.1 %**) |
    71	//! | [`tc91_duplicate_embed_rate_anonymous`] | same, `logical_id: None` | 10 | 103-110 / 200 (**52.8 %**) |
    72	//! | [`tc91_control_unblocked_worker_commits`] | ONE 96-row batch, no concurrent writer | 10 | **0 / 96** |
    73	//! | [`tc91_forced_commit_failure_is_invisible_to_terminal_counting`] | same batch + held write lock | 10 | 80-96 / 96 |
    74	//! | [`tc91_mechanism_duplicate_rate_versus_write_cadence`] | 60 rows, 1 ms vs 25 ms apart | 10 | 31-33 vs **0** |
    75	//!
    76	//! Governed and anonymous are indistinguishable, confirming the ledger claim that
    77	//! this is not the TC-57 race.
    78	//!
    79	//! ## What those numbers DO and DO NOT prove — read this before quoting them
    80	//!
    81	//! Two claims are **measured**, and they are the deliverable:
    82	//!
    83	//! 1. The duplicate rate is **CADENCE-SENSITIVE** under this 1 ms embed / 1 ms
    84	//!    write shape: 32.5/60 at 1 ms spacing, **0/60 at 25 ms**, 10/10, and 0/96 for
    85	//!    a single batch with no concurrent writer. So the ~50 % is a property of the
    86	//!    write **cadence**, not a constant of the scheduler, and quoting it without
    87	//!    the cadence it was measured at is meaningless.
    88	//! 2. An **externally held WAL write lock makes discarded worker commits
    89	//!    invisible**: 86.4/96 duplicates while `'failed'` terminals and
    90	//!    `projection_failures` rows are both ZERO, 10/10.
    91	//!
    92	//! The tempting third claim — *"(a) IS (b)"*, i.e. that a duplicate REQUIRES a
    93	//! second writer holding the WAL write lock across the worker's commit — is a
    94	//! **HYPOTHESIS strongly implicated by (1) + (2), not a measured identity**
    95	//! (codex §9 round 4 finding 4). The gap is specific and worth naming, because it
    96	//! is not an oversight:
    97	//!
    98	//! > **There is no direct instrumentation of worker commit failures in the
    99	//! > baseline duplicate arms — because the defect under study is precisely that
   100	//! > those failures are discarded by `let _ =`.** TC-91 **(b) is the reason
   101	//! > TC-91 (a) cannot be closed by measurement.** The forced-lock experiment
   102	//! > reproduces the mechanism, but it supplies the lock holder externally; it
   103	//! > cannot show that every baseline duplicate came from the engine's own writer.
   104	//!
   105	//! The supporting code reading is real — `commit_batch` holds `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`
   106	//! (`lib.rs:18637`, the TC-57 fix) while `commit_projection_outcomes`
   107	//! (`lib.rs:13809`) is a DEFERRED read-then-upgrade — but it is a reading, and
   108	//! §0 of the TC-57 characterization is the standing warning about confident
   109	//! readings on this exact code path. Landing **R-C** (stop discarding the commit
   110	//! result) is what would convert the hypothesis into a measurement; that is the
   111	//! main reason R-C is recommended FIRST in the design doc §7.
   112	//!
   113	//! In EVERY one of the 50 runs above, `failed_terminals == 0` and
   114	//! `failure_audit_rows == 0`.
   115	//!
   116	//! ## Test profile — which targets run in the DEFAULT `cargo test` gate
   117	//!
   118	//! Decided deliberately per target, not by reflex (codex §9 round 4 finding 3).
   119	//! The gating question is TC-72: roughly 1 workspace run in 3 already fails on
   120	//! plain `main`, so anything timing-sensitive added to the default profile makes
   121	//! that worse and must earn its place.
   122	//!
   123	//! **`#[ignore]`d — every arm whose result is a RATE measured under concurrency:**
   124	//! [`tc91_duplicate_embed_rate_governed`], [`tc91_duplicate_embed_rate_anonymous`],
   125	//! [`tc91_mechanism_duplicate_rate_versus_write_cadence`],
   126	//! [`tc91_forced_commit_failure_is_invisible_to_terminal_counting`],
   127	//! [`tc91_control_unblocked_worker_commits`]. Each races a live projection worker
   128	//! against a live writer; their baseline values are MEASUREMENTS, not merge-gate
   129	//! invariants. Run them with `--ignored`.
   130	//!
   131	//! **LIVE in the default profile — one target:**
   132	//! [`tc91_probe_embed_cost_per_open_at_head`]. It is a measurement too, but it is
   133	//! **sequential and deterministic**: three opens of one workspace, one write, every
   134	//! `drain` awaited to idle, no second thread and no wall-clock assertion anywhere.
   135	//! Its value comes from a checked-in fixture (`vector_equivalence_probes.txt`) and
   136	//! a code path, so it has no run-to-run variance to flake on — and since it now
   137	//! pins `0 / 90 / 0` exactly, leaving it live is what actually defends the
   138	//! correction the design doc publishes. Gating it would put the corrected figure
   139	//! behind a flag nobody runs.
   140	
   141	use fathomdb_embedder_api::{Embedder, EmbedderError, EmbedderIdentity, Vector};
   142	use fathomdb_engine::{Engine, InitialState, PreparedWrite, SourceId};
   143	use fathomdb_schema::SQLITE_SUFFIX;
   144	use std::collections::HashMap;
   145	use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
   146	use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
   147	use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
   148	use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
   149	use tempfile::TempDir;
   150	
   151	/// Present in every body this fixture writes and in no probe text, so ingest
   152	/// embeds and probe embeds can be told apart WITHIN one open rather than inferred
   153	/// from a total.
   154	const INGEST_MARKER: &str = "tc91marker";
   155	
   156	/// Rows per duplicate-rate arm.
   157	const ROWS: usize = 200;
   158	
   159	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   160	// Fixture
   161	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   162	
   163	/// Records the exact `text` of every `embed()` call.
   164	///
   165	/// A text embedded twice is a row that was DISPATCHED twice — i.e. an outcome
   166	/// that was computed and then never became durable. Every row this fixture writes
   167	/// carries a unique body, which is what makes "duplicate" a falsifiable count
   168	/// rather than an inference from a total.
   169	#[derive(Debug)]
   170	struct RecordingEmbedder {
   171	    identity: EmbedderIdentity,
   172	    calls: Arc<AtomicUsize>,
   173	    texts: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<String, usize>>>,
   174	    delay: Duration,
   175	}
   176	
   177	impl RecordingEmbedder {
   178	    fn new(log: &EmbedLog, delay: Duration) -> Self {
   179	        Self {
   180	            identity: EmbedderIdentity::new("deterministic", "rev-a", 384),
   181	            calls: Arc::clone(&log.calls),
   182	            texts: Arc::clone(&log.texts),
   183	            delay,
   184	        }
   185	    }
   186	}
   187	
   188	impl Embedder for RecordingEmbedder {
   189	    fn identity(&self) -> EmbedderIdentity {
   190	        self.identity.clone()
   191	    }
   192	
   193	    fn embed(&self, text: &str) -> Result<Vector, EmbedderError> {
   194	        self.calls.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
   195	        *self
   196	            .texts
   197	            .lock()
   198	            .unwrap_or_else(|poisoned| poisoned.into_inner())
   199	            .entry(text.to_string())
   200	            .or_insert(0) += 1;
   201	        if !self.delay.is_zero() {
   202	            std::thread::sleep(self.delay);
   203	        }
   204	        let mut v = vec![0.0_f32; self.identity.dimension as usize];
   205	        v[0] = 1.0;
   206	        Ok(v)
   207	    }
   208	}
   209	
   210	/// Shared embed log, so the counts survive the embedder being moved into the
   211	/// engine.
   212	#[derive(Clone, Default)]
   213	struct EmbedLog {
   214	    calls: Arc<AtomicUsize>,
   215	    texts: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<String, usize>>>,
   216	}
   217	
   218	/// Ingest / probe split of an embed log, with duplicates counted per class.
   219	#[derive(Debug, Default, Clone, Copy)]
   220	struct EmbedSplit {
   221	    /// Total `embed()` calls whose text carries [`INGEST_MARKER`].
   222	    ingest_calls: usize,
   223	    /// Distinct ingest texts — i.e. rows reached at least once.
   224	    ingest_distinct: usize,
   225	    /// Ingest embeds beyond the first for any text. THE duplicate count.
   226	    ingest_duplicates: usize,
   227	    /// Distinct ingest texts embedded more than once.
   228	    ingest_repeated_texts: usize,
   229	    /// Total `embed()` calls whose text does NOT carry [`INGEST_MARKER`] — the
   230	    /// vector-equivalence probe, reported SEPARATELY and never summed in.
   231	    probe_calls: usize,
   232	}
   233	
   234	impl EmbedLog {
   235	    fn total(&self) -> usize {
   236	        self.calls.load(Ordering::SeqCst)
   237	    }
   238	
   239	    fn split(&self) -> EmbedSplit {
   240	        let texts = self.texts.lock().unwrap_or_else(|poisoned| poisoned.into_inner());
   241	        let mut split = EmbedSplit::default();
   242	        for (text, n) in texts.iter() {
   243	            if text.contains(INGEST_MARKER) {
   244	                split.ingest_calls += n;
   245	                split.ingest_distinct += 1;
   246	                split.ingest_duplicates += n.saturating_sub(1);
   247	                if *n > 1 {
   248	                    split.ingest_repeated_texts += 1;
   249	                }
   250	            } else {
   251	                split.probe_calls += n;
   252	            }
   253	        }
   254	        split
   255	    }
   256	}
