dev/todos-and-considerations-ledger.jsonl:125:{"ts": "2026-07-28T20:51:48.945Z", "seq": 125, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TC-71 CLOSED and LANDED in Slice 21 (77be504b). vector_projection_declared now requires the searchable role - and codex round 1 caught that gating the DECISION without reconciling ALREADY-ENROLLED state left the entire harm intact on upgrade.", "area": "engine", "decider": "steward", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-71", "priority": "p1", "status": "closed", "refs": ["git:77be504b", "git:3349344b", "tc:TC-87"], "body": "Left OPEN at the Slice 20c five-round circuit breaker, closed here. THE BASE FIX: the predicate keyed off the stored vector sub-object alone, so {roles:[filterable], vector:true} activated the dense arm instead of staying inert. It now answers through the typed registry (roles is persisted as a comma-joined sorted string and is NOT a trustworthy SQL predicate - a LIKE would match a forward-compat token that merely CONTAINS a role spelling). Re-verified across all three paths the predicate gates: forward backfill, drop inverse, late enrolment. The RED test runs with a LIVE embedder, because the shipped inertness test passes VACUOUSLY without one. THE FINDING THAT MATTERED (codex section 9 round 1, the single distinct finding of the review): the base fix corrected the DECISION to enrol but not the ALREADY-ENROLLED STATE. lib.rs:17550 gates the enqueue solely on _fathomdb_vector_kinds membership, and vector_kind_needs_enrolment tests kind_is_vector_indexed BEFORE reaching the new predicate, so an already-enrolled kind never sees the fix; the only un-enrol path fires on a before-and-not-after transition. Net: a database that had already run the old code KEPT EMBEDDING under inert declarations - TC-71's entire stated harm surviving its own fix, for exactly the users who already had it. Fixed at open time (RED f70f00bf, GREEN 3349344b), not overridden. TWO IMPLEMENTER CORRECTIONS TO THE ORCHESTRATOR'S OWN BRIEF, both load-bearing: (1) the legacy-database safety argument was WRONG - schema step 24 CREATES the registry table on every open, so a legacy DB boots with the table present but EMPTY and the table-exists probe never protects it; EXISTS(vector_declared = 1) is the sole protection, audited against the four at-risk suites; (2) reconciliation in apply_projection_config had to be 'before OR predicate', not a replacement - the predicate alone would have REGRESSED the shipped drop case, since dropping the last projection carrying a vector sub-object leaves the EXISTS condition false. Neither implies the other. ALSO: absolute embed counts are unusable as an oracle across a reopen, because run_vector_equivalence_probe re-embeds 45 references on any open with a non-empty _fathomdb_vector_kinds; every embed assertion is now a DELTA from a post-open snapshot."}
dev/todos-and-considerations-ledger.jsonl:129:{"ts": "2026-07-28T22:13:33.510Z", "seq": 129, "kind": "observation", "summary": "Two further vector/worker defects carried forward from Slice 21, both unfixed and unowned: (a) the ~50 percent BASELINE duplicate-embed rate, present in the ANONYMOUS arm too so NOT TC-57's doing, and (b) projection-worker commit failures silently discarded via 'let _ = commit_projection_outcomes(..)'.", "area": "engine", "decider": "steward", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-91", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["tc:TC-57", "tc:TC-68", "git:77be504b"], "body": "Both surfaced during the Slice 21 investigation, both correctly left out of scope, both recorded in board section 18 - which is a close record, not a work queue, so they are lifted here. (a) DUPLICATE EMBEDS: roughly half of embeds at BASELINE are duplicates. Critically this reproduces in the ANONYMOUS arm as well, so it is NOT caused by the governed-write race TC-57 fixed and did not go away with it. Impact is wasted embed work - the same harm class as TC-71, at a different layer - and it is invisible to correctness tests because a duplicate embed produces the right answer, just twice. Note the interaction with TC-68 (Slice 22): run_vector_equivalence_probe already re-embeds 45 references on ANY open with a non-empty _fathomdb_vector_kinds, which is a separate known source of repeat embedding that TC-68 is scheduled to cache away; whoever takes this must separate the two before attributing a rate. (b) SILENTLY DISCARDED WORKER COMMIT FAILURES: the projection worker's commit path is invoked as 'let _ = commit_projection_outcomes(..)', so a failure to commit projection outcomes is dropped on the floor with no error, no log and no telemetry. Consequence: the worker can fail to persist its work and nothing anywhere reports it - and, as the Slice 21 orchestrator found the hard way, it also means measuring worker risk via 'failed' terminal states is STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE of seeing this class, because the failure never reaches a terminal state at all. That second point is the durable one: any future attempt to quantify worker reliability by counting failed terminals will report a falsely clean number until this is fixed. (c) ALSO CARRIED, lower priority: TC-57 remedy R2 (bounded retry on the busy codes) remains unimplemented pending an error-path restructure; R1/BEGIN IMMEDIATE shipped instead and closed the measured defect, so R2 is an option not an obligation - see the five-remedy enumeration in dev/design/0.8.20-tc57-write-race-characterization.md section 7."}
dev/todos-and-considerations-ledger.jsonl:134:{"ts": "2026-07-29T02:27:01.478Z", "seq": 134, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TC-68 CLOSED in Slice 22 (572475f2) - and the commissions OWN DoD CLAUSE WAS VACUOUS. \"Open cost independent of enrolled-kind count\" was ALREADY TRUE before the slice (the gate is SELECT EXISTS, not a count). The real cost was measured instead: 90 embeds on the population open, 45 on EVERY open thereafter, forever.", "area": "engine", "decider": "steward", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-68", "priority": "p2", "status": "closed", "refs": ["git:572475f2", "tc:TC-10"], "body": "A DoD clause that is already satisfied at baseline is a vacuous gate - the TC-37 class, and it was written by the Steward into the R-20-VC row and carried into the commission unchallenged. The orchestrator caught it and re-derived a real measurement rather than passing a test that could not fail. RECORD THE REAL NUMBERS: both figures previously in the ledger were true, but of DIFFERENT opens - 90 embeds on the population open, 45 on every subsequent open, permanently, because run_vector_equivalence_probe re-embeds 45 reference probes on ANY open with a non-empty _fathomdb_vector_kinds. THE FIX: a fingerprint keyed on the embedder identity triple PLUS the live mean_vec bytes AND the stored baseline blobs - deliberately not the identity alone, and never the embedder NAME alone, which would silently fail to re-fire on a revision or dimension change and defeat the guarantee TC-68 was ruled to PRESERVE. Stored in the EXISTING _fathomdb_open_state; NO schema bump, SCHEMA stays 24. NOTE A GAP THE SLICE COULD NOT CLOSE: EmbedderIdentity carries NO normalization field, so mean_vec covers that axis in practice rather than by construction. A backend/device descriptor on EmbedderIdentity is the only thing that RETIRES this residual - recorded separately."}
dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-22-fix-2-output.json:39:      "probe_verification_fingerprint (src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs:15471) hashes: recipe tag (build const), identity.name/revision/dimension, mean-centering flag + live mean_vec bytes, VECTOR_EQUIVALENCE_PROBE_FIXTURE (build const), the two floors (build consts), and the stored _fathomdb_embed_probe rows. A same-identity backend swap moves none of them: the triple is unchanged by definition (and check_embedder_profile refuses the open outright if it changes), mean_vec/baseline live in the DB and are untouched by swapping backends, the rest are compiled in.",
dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-22-fix-2-output.json:45:    "precision_note": "The window is not only 'fresh databases'. It is three cases, enumerated in design-note §8.5: (1) never verified under the current fingerprint, incl. a pre-TC-68 DB — with the empty-probe-table sub-case where forgery is pointless because probe_populate_or_check baselines against whatever backend is live (pre-existing 0.8.18 behaviour, verified at lib.rs:15366-15375); (2) the open right after a LEGITIMATE fingerprint change (recomputed mean_vec, fixture edit, recipe bump), which an honest open would re-verify and at which the drift WOULD be caught; (3) an open after a failing verdict cleared the marker. Case 2 is the one that matters: it is the only recurring detection opportunity §6 left standing, and a forged marker removes it."
