dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-22-fix-1-output.json:16:    "lib.rs: probe_verification_is_cached rustdoc — the over-claim replaced by what a `true` actually means, plus the threat-model pointer. No behaviour change anywhere.",
dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-22-fix-1-output.json:21:  "next_step_for_orchestrator": "HYPOTHESIS HELD — proven by tests/tc68_probe_fingerprint_cache.rs::a_forged_stored_baseline_defeats_the_probe_even_when_it_fully_runs. Re-baselining every _fathomdb_embed_probe.reference_vec to the drifted backend's own output (ordinals/texts/identity/blob-lengths preserved, so the 0.8.18 fix-2 completeness validation sees nothing), with the cache marker DELETED, makes the probe run all 45 embeds and PASS on a drifted arm: dense stays enabled and search does not refuse. The control leg in the same test (identical drift, un-forged baseline, marker cleared) IS caught, so the forgery is the cause. The body exercised is byte-identical to the slice baseline fe85337f — `git diff fe85337f a39b865e -- src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs` shows the only edits inside probe_check_against_baseline_inner are the doc comment, a type alias, the cache gate and the record_probe_verification call. Therefore the cache adds NO new attack surface and this is NOT a regression. CODE vs DOCS: no behaviour changed at all — the only lib.rs edit is rustdoc. Everything substantive is documentation (design note §5 correction + new §8, CHANGELOG, three interfaces docs) plus 4 characterization/pin tests added to the suite the slice gate already runs. HARDENING CONSIDERED AND REJECTED (design note §8.5): (a) salt/MAC/keyed digest — no secret exists in an embedded local-first engine; a salt is readable by the same actor; (b) obfuscating the marker — stops nobody who can read the schema and leaves the shorter baseline-forgery route open; theatre; (c) a DELETE-the-marker trigger on _fathomdb_embed_probe — DDL, so it needs a migration step + SCHEMA_VERSION bump (prohibited for this slice) AND buys nothing, because the baseline forgery WANTS the probe to run; (d) folding in an input a naive external writer would forget — no such input exists that is not already a fingerprint input, and inventing one (write counter, mtime) re-opens the §6.2 non-determinism objection. DEVIATION, stated loudly: there is NO RED commit. The finding is a false claim in prose, not a behavioural regression, so no test can be made to fail at HEAD without duplicating the fingerprint recipe in a test (which would make VECTOR_EQUIVALENCE_FINGERPRINT_RECIPE un-bumpable) or grepping design-doc prose from the engine test suite. The RED slot is instead a measurement commit (d348e4b5) whose message states this explicitly; the reviewer should judge the pair on that basis. NOTHING ELSE in the slice was touched: Risks 1/3/4 came back PASS and their code is unmodified. Suggested next: re-run codex §9 on the round-2 finding only, then cherry-pick d348e4b5 + 73656165."
