dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-22-partB-output.json:37:  "next_step_for_orchestrator": "TC-68 COMPLETE at 17951f05 (3 commits: b3769047 RED, 88f941fd GREEN, 17951f05 docs). Part A's legs (validate_write, delete_vector_partition_row, erase_row_projections, the two part-A design docs) are byte-untouched; `configure_projections` / `apply_projection_config` / `enqueue_declared_vector_backfill` / `ProjectionDelta` are untouched (part C). `output.json` untouched. || MEASURED BASELINE (counting embedder, `embed` calls snapshotted across `Engine::open`, engine closed between opens, no rows written): ZERO enrolled kinds = 0 embeds on every open. ONE enrolled kind = 90 / 45 / 45 across opens 1-3. SIX enrolled kinds = 90 / 45 / 45 — IDENTICAL. So 45 AND 90 are BOTH true, of different opens: 90 is the ONE-TIME population open (45 in `probe_populate_baseline` to persist the references + 45 more because `probe_populate_or_check` immediately runs the fix-2 'confirm the just-written baseline' check), and 45 is EVERY open thereafter, forever. The ledger's '90 synchronous embeds' and the fixture's 45 were being quoted about different opens; neither was wrong. The population open is deliberately LEFT at 90 (shrinking it would trade the fix-2 guard for a one-off saving). || FINGERPRINT INPUTS, exactly: SHA-256 over length-prefixed fields — (1) recipe tag `fathomdb-veq-verdict-v1`; (2) identity.name / .revision / .dimension [defence-in-depth ONLY — `check_embedder_profile` refuses the open first, so unreachable here]; (3) whether mean-centering applies + the live pinned `mean_vec` bytes [LOAD-BEARING: P1 quantizes sign(x - mean_vec), and open-time mean-recovery/requantize and the operator `recompute_mean` verb both rewrite it, so an identity-triple-only key would be stale by construction]; (4) `VECTOR_EQUIVALENCE_PROBE_FIXTURE`; (5) `VECTOR_EQUIVALENCE_P1_FLIP_FLOOR` and `VECTOR_EQUIVALENCE_L2_EPSILON`; (6) the stored baseline rows INCLUDING the reference blobs. On the fixture-digest question you asked me to decide: the drift test does NOT make it redundant (it pins the engine copy equal to the embedder-crate copy — COPY drift between two committed files, not content across releases). The fix-2 completeness check does independently fail CLOSED first on a fixture edit, so the digest is belt-and-braces; I kept it anyway because it costs one hash of a ~3 KB constant and makes the fingerprint a self-contained statement of what the verdict depended on rather than relying on a second mechanism failing closed first. Reasoning in design note §2.3. || STORAGE: one row in `_fathomdb_open_state(key,value)` — migration step 1, the engine's existing open-time durable-marker KV table alongside `projection_cursor`, `search_index_tokenizer_reproject_complete`, `tc33_edge_vector_prune_complete`, `tc33_reserved_write_cursor`. Key = `vector_equivalence_verified_fingerprint`. So NO `SCHEMA_VERSION` bump, NO migration step, and not even the `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`-at-open route you suggested — the table already exists on every migrated DB. No schema-drift assertion is affected: the schema crate's step tests assert per-step DDL and index shape, none enumerates `_fathomdb_open_state` ROWS; `cargo test -p fathomdb-schema` green. || THE RESIDUAL, one sentence: a backend that drifts WITHOUT changing its declared identity (candle CPU<->CUDA, a rebuilt library/driver) moves no fingerprint input, so the cached verdict answers and the drift is no longer caught on any open — it is caught only at the next open whose fingerprint does change, which by definition a pure backend swap is not. || SDK TEST FILES TOUCHED: NONE, in any language — and this is a deliberate, reasoned decision I am flagging loudly rather than an omission. TC-68 adds no public surface (no type, field, method or error), the X1 probe suites (`src/python/tests/test_vector_equivalence_probe.py`, `src/ts/tests/vector-equivalence-probe.test.ts`) are surface-parity-only by their own docstrings and explicitly defer divergence behaviour to the Rust engine suite, AND the probe is unreachable from a default SDK session (no live embedder without `use_default_embedder` / `useDefaultEmbedder`, which needs weights/network). Any mirror I wrote would have been green without exercising the cache — the exact vacuous-green trap you warned about. I documented the one consumer-observable semantic shift in `dev/interfaces/{rust,python,typescript}.md` and CHANGELOG.md instead. If you want the live harness run anyway, there is nothing new to run; if you disagree with the call, the honest non-vacuous SDK test would require an SDK session with a real embedder, which no existing SDK test has. || FOR YOUR SIGN-OFF QUEUE (§3.5 — described, NOT implemented): the ONE change that would genuinely retire the residual is widening `EmbedderIdentity` (or the `Embedder` trait) with a RUNTIME BACKEND/DEVICE DESCRIPTOR — e.g. `candle-cuda:driver=12.4`, or a build/feature fingerprint — and folding it into the verdict fingerprint. `EmbedderIdentity` is `{name, revision, dimension}` today with no such field and the trait reports nothing else, so this is public-surface work on a governed type and needs HITL sign-off. It belongs with the batched governed-surface decision the plan places AFTER Slice 22. Recorded in design note §6.2; NOT taken here. || OTHER DEVIATIONS, stated loudly: (1) I modified the shipped `vector_equivalence_probe.rs` — 44 lines, adding a documented `force_probe_verdict_rerun` helper and 4 call sites. Without it 12 of its 23 tests would have gone VACUOUS (asserting a divergence is caught on an open where the probe never ran). No assertion was relaxed. This is the one place a reviewer should look hardest. (2) I split `probe_check_against_baseline` into a thin wrapper + `_inner` so every failure path clears the cache; the wrapper is the only new control flow in the shipped path. || SUGGESTED REVIEWER FOCUS for codex §9: (a) is the cache gate's placement after the fix-2 completeness validation genuinely sufficient to preserve the external-tamper closure, given the blob digest is in the fingerprint; (b) is swallowing a cache-WRITE failure right (I argue yes — the arm was just verified, and the consequence is the next open re-runs, i.e. more work not less); (c) does `force_probe_verdict_rerun` in the shipped suite hide anything a reviewer would want asserted differently."
