dev/plans/runs/0.8.20-slice-22-partC-output.json:23:  "next_step_for_orchestrator": "TC-67 landed as commits 34b13c61 (RED) / 423e5f22 (GREEN) / 65017319 (X1) / a39b865e (docs). HEAD = a39b865e.\n\n1. FIELD SHAPE AND NAME. `ProjectionDelta.vector_unsupported_kinds: Vec<String>` (engine + pyo3 + Python SDK); `vectorUnsupportedKinds: string[]` (napi + TS SDK). It is a list of node KINDS, sorted and de-duplicated (SELECT DISTINCT ... ORDER BY kind), EMPTY rather than absent, OUTPUT-ONLY. Named for the AXIS: the three sibling vectors are projection ATTRIBUTE NAMES, this one is node KINDS, so the name says `kinds` out loud; the `vector_` prefix binds it to the dense arm because a reported kind is still fully FTS/lexically searchable (without it the field reads as 'kinds FathomDB does not support', which is false and alarming); 'unsupported' rather than 'skipped'/'failed' because nothing was retried and nothing errored. Kept a Vec<String> rather than a Vec<{kind, reason}> struct: there is exactly ONE reason a kind can appear, so a reason field would be a single-variant enum, and a struct would mean a net-new #[napi(object)] plus a net-new #[pyclass] on the slice you flagged as most likely to add surface. Both SDK suites assert the negative in both directions (no attribute name in the report, no kind in `deferred`) so the two axes cannot later be merged silently.\n\n2. READINESS SEMANTICS ARE UNCHANGED — confirmed, and pinned by a dedicated test in each language: Rust `readiness_semantics_are_unchanged_by_the_report` (tests/tc67_unsupported_vector_kind_report.rs), Python `test_readiness_semantics_are_unchanged_by_the_report`, TS `readiness semantics are unchanged by the report`. Deliberately separated from the report test and built on the harshest fixture — a corpus of ONLY unsupported kinds with the dense arm declared — because the tempting 'fix' (treat an unsupported kind as unfinished work) would satisfy a report-only assertion while wedging every consumer whose corpus contains one. It asserts dense_readiness == Ready and that drain returns, then writes MORE unsupported rows and re-asserts. slice20_dense_readiness (8/8) and slice20c_flush_barrier (12/12) are also green.\n\n3. DECLARE-TIME RESIDUAL — I took OPTION (a): the report is computed at declare time from the kinds already in canonical_nodes, so a non-committable kind written LATER is absent from a delta the caller already holds; the documented refresh is to re-apply the same spec, an idempotent no-op that returns a current report (which works only because the report is state-keyed, not diff-keyed, and does not feed `unchanged`). Option (b) was rejected because read_projections returns Vec<ProjectionSpec> and ProjectionSpec is the CALLER-AUTHORED declaration type: hanging the report there would need either a second accept-inert field (the dense_readiness treatment) on a type whose whole contract is 'what you declared', or a change to read_projections' return type — both larger and more governed than the fact warrants. Documented in the design note §5, in all three dev/interfaces docs, and in CHANGELOG.\n\n4. §11 ITEM 4 (searchable-role rejection) — NOT implemented, per your instruction, and my work makes it MARGINALLY EASIER, not harder. The rejection belongs in apply_projection_config's up-front validation loop beside the existing empty-roles and duplicate-name refusals — same loop, a fourth `if`, before any write. TC-67 added nothing to that loop and nothing to ProjectionSpec; it only appends a field to the returned delta on the `vector_declared_after` path, which runs long after validation. The one assist: tests/tc67_unsupported_vector_kind_report.rs already builds both spec shapes (`vector_spec` / `filterable_only_spec`) with helpers a rejection suite can lift verbatim, and slice21c_vector_role_gate.rs already carries `filterable_vector_spec` — the exact shape to be rejected. Reasoning recorded in design note §7.\n\n5. SDK FILES TOUCHED, by language.\n   Rust (engine): src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs (field + vector_eligible_node_kinds + unsupported_vector_kinds + the apply_projection_config assignment); src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/tc67_unsupported_vector_kind_report.rs (new).\n   Rust (bindings): src/rust/crates/fathomdb-py/src/lib.rs (PyProjectionDelta + from_rust); src/rust/crates/fathomdb-napi/src/lib.rs (ProjectionDelta + from_rust).\n   Python SDK: src/python/fathomdb/types.py (dataclass field, default_factory=list); src/python/fathomdb/engine.py (delta construction); src/python/fathomdb/_fathomdb.pyi (stub); src/python/tests/test_tc67_unsupported_vector_kind_report.py (new, 6 arms).\n   TypeScript SDK: src/ts/src/index.ts (public ProjectionDelta interface + doc); src/ts/src/binding.ts (NativeProjectionDelta); src/ts/tests/tc67-unsupported-vector-kind-report.test.ts (new, 6 arms).\n   The facade crate src/rust/crates/fathomdb/src/lib.rs needed NO change (it re-exports the type, not its fields).\n   NOTE for your build step: five of the six SDK arms need NO embedder (that is itself the property under test — the report is embedder-independent), so they run fast and offline. Only the readiness arm opens with use_default_embedder=True / { useDefaultEmbedder: true } and it honours FATHOMDB_SKIP_NETWORK_TESTS.\n\n6. GOVERNED-SURFACE PIN — rc=0, allowlist sha 555e6ae7f7d13e537170dbd63eed4a0433a82a0f7bdc03781ec4e0ef7692c02b BYTE-IDENTICAL, exactly as you expected: the allowlist governs COMMANDS, not struct fields, so an additive field on ProjectionDelta is invisible to it (the same outcome Slice 20 saw with dense_readiness). Run EARLY (immediately after the first binding edit) as instructed, and again at closure. Neither JSON file was touched by any commit. SCHEMA_VERSION still 24, no migration step.\n\n7. DEVIATIONS — one, stated loudly and I judge it in-scope rather than creep: I extracted `vector_eligible_node_kinds(tx)` out of enqueue_declared_vector_backfill instead of writing a second SELECT for the report. The alternative was two hand-copied queries over canonical_nodes, which is the TC-56 drift shape and would drift in the worst direction here — a report describing a different set from the one actually excluded. The extraction is behaviour-identical apart from an added `ORDER BY kind` (which only fixes the ordering the report needs; enrolment is order-insensitive, INSERT OR IGNORE). slice20c_flush_barrier and slice21c_vector_role_gate, which own that code path, are green. No other deviation. I did NOT touch validate_write, delete_vector_partition_row, erase_row_projections, run_vector_equivalence_probe or its cache, dev/design/0.8.20-tc68-*.md, dev/design/errors.md, dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md or dev/plans/runs/STATUS-0.8.20.md. output.json and output-partB.json are untouched and still untracked.\n\n8. FOR YOUR SQLITE-VEC FOLLOW-UP: the doc now records that part A's tripwire `bare_vec0_delete_pins_the_upstream_length_boundary` WILL go RED on the 0.1.9 bump, by design, and that its assertion message already tells the reader to delete the delete_vector_partition_row neutralize step. Nothing in Cargo.toml / Cargo.lock / the =0.1.7 pins was changed (verified by an empty `git diff fe85337f -- ...` over those paths)."
