dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-22-fix-1-rereview-20260729T011134Z.log:16:CONTEXT. Your own previous round raised exactly one finding, a [P1]: 'Forged TC-68 cache value can skip the probe and fail open' — probe_verification_is_cached trusts any _fathomdb_open_state.value equal to the freshly computed SHA-256, and because every fingerprint input is deterministic and publicly derivable from the database, a writer can forge the digest and skip the 45-probe verification.
dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-22-fix-1-rereview-20260729T011134Z.log:18:THE RESPONSE, which you must now judge. The implementer did NOT attempt cryptographic authentication (no secret exists in an embedded local-first engine; a salt is readable by the same actor). Instead it asserted, and claims to have PROVEN by test, that this is a PRE-EXISTING property rather than a new weakness: an actor who can write the database can equally re-baseline _fathomdb_embed_probe.reference_vec to a drifted backend's own output, so the probe passes on a drifted arm even when it FULLY RUNS with no cache involved. The claimed proof is the test a_forged_stored_baseline_defeats_the_probe_even_when_it_fully_runs, which forges the baseline, DELETES the cache marker, and shows the drifted backend is served; with a control leg (same drift, un-forged baseline, marker cleared) that IS caught.
