dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-22-fix-2-rereview-20260729T014025Z.log:16:YOUR OWN FINDING, twice raised: [P1] the TC-68 fingerprint cache lets a database writer forge one publicly-computable digest and skip the 45-probe verification, and re-baselining the 45 _fathomdb_embed_probe.reference_vec blobs to a drifted backend's exact outputs is a MATERIALLY HIGHER BAR — so the doc claim 'the cache adds no new attack surface' was an over-claim.
dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-22-fix-2-rereview-20260729T014025Z.log:22:CLAIM: an attacker who wants a drifted backend served does not need to forge anything at all. A same-identity backend drift moves NO fingerprint input (probe_verification_fingerprint hashes only: a recipe tag, the identity triple, the mean-centering flag plus live mean_vec bytes, the compiled-in fixture, the two D4 floors, and the stored _fathomdb_embed_probe rows). So a marker recorded honestly by an earlier open still matches after the swap, the probe is skipped, and the drifted backend is served with NO forgery. That is the RULED, already-documented residual (design note §6), not a new defect. Test: residual_same_identity_backend_drift_is_not_caught_on_a_cached_open, strengthened to read the engine's own marker before the drifted open and assert it byte-unchanged after.
