   372	|---|---|---|
   373	| the 4 `tc90_*` loop arms | **`#[ignore]`d** | race a live worker against live writer threads; results are RATES, and §2.2 shows the per-run count is not even stable session to session |
   374	| the 5 `tc91_*` concurrency arms | **`#[ignore]`d** | same — live worker vs live writer, measured rates |
   375	| `tc90_mechanism_transition_sql_shape_on_real_schema_is_busy_5` | **LIVE** | two connections, **one thread**, no sleeps; the lock is HELD, not raced |
   376	| `tc90_mechanism_engine_transition_under_held_write_lock_fails_immediately` | **LIVE** | blocker held on the calling thread; no outstanding `drain` work |
   377	| `tc90_mechanism_control_engine_write_under_held_write_lock_survives` | **LIVE** | the one target with a thread — see below |
   378	| `tc91_probe_embed_cost_per_open_at_head` | **LIVE** | sequential; 3 opens, 1 write, every `drain` awaited; no thread, no wall-clock assertion |
   379	
   380	Running the mechanism pins live is the same posture TC-57's own `tc57_mechanism_*` pins have.
