   543	Both discard a `rusqlite::Result<()>`. No error, no log, no telemetry, no counter.
   544	
   545	**The durable point — state it once, plainly, because it invalidates a whole class of future
   546	measurement.** A `'failed'` terminal and a `projection_failures` audit row are written by the
   547	`ProjectionOutcome::Failure` arm (`lib.rs:13962-13984`), which represents an **embed** failure. A
   548	**commit** failure never reaches that arm at all: the transaction rolls back, the row keeps
   549	`terminal IS NULL`, the worker loop sets `pending_scan = true` (`lib.rs:12663`), the dispatcher
   550	re-fetches the row and it is embedded again.
   551	
   552	> **Counting `'failed'` terminal states is STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE of seeing this failure class.**
   553	> The failure never reaches a terminal state at all. Any future attempt to quantify worker
   554	> reliability by counting failed terminals — or `projection_failures` rows — will report a
   555	> **falsely clean zero**, no matter how many commits are being lost, until this is fixed.
   556	
   557	**Forced-failure experiment.** `tc91_forced_commit_failure_is_invisible_to_terminal_counting`
   558	writes one 96-row governed batch with a 15 ms embedder, then takes and **holds** the WAL write
   559	lock from an ordinary second connection (`BEGIN IMMEDIATE`) for 1 500 ms. That is precisely the
   560	contention §3.2 characterizes, applied in the opposite direction: `commit_projection_outcomes`
   561	(`lib.rs:13809`) opens rusqlite's DEFERRED default and reads (`lib.rs:13813`, `lib.rs:13828`)
   562	before it writes (`lib.rs:13863`), so its promotion is refused and `let _ =` throws the outcome
   563	away.
   564	
   565	| observable | blocked arm (N=10) | unblocked control (N=10) |
   566	|---|---|---|
   567	| duplicate embeds / 96 rows | 80–96, mean **86.4** (**90 %**) | **0** |
   568	| distinct rows re-embedded | **32** in every run | 0 |
   569	| **`'failed'` terminals** | **0** | **0** |
   570	| **`projection_failures` rows** | **0** | **0** |
   571	| `up_to_date` terminals | 96 | 96 |
   572	| `vector_rows` | 96 | 96 |
   573	| `drain` reached idle | 10/10 | 10/10 |
   574	
   575	Both arms passed 10/10 — because the test **asserts the blindness**: it requires the two
   576	counters to stay at **zero** while the re-embed count is **non-zero**. A green run therefore
   577	means *real work was computed, thrown away and recomputed, and the terminal-state census
   578	reported a perfectly clean run.* The control's job is to show the same two counters read zero
   579	for the **ordinary** reason (nothing failed) — which is exactly why they cannot discriminate,
   580	and exactly why a future agent must not use them.
   581	
   582	Note also: **`vector_rows == 96` in the blocked arm.** This is a **wasted-work / observability**
   583	defect, not data loss. The system self-heals; it just does so silently and at up to double the
   584	embed cost.
   585	
   586	### 5.5 The mechanism — what is MEASURED, and where the evidence structurally stops
   587	
   588	`tc91_mechanism_duplicate_rate_versus_write_cadence` runs the same 60 governed rows twice,
   589	varying **only** the spacing between `Engine::write` calls.
   590	
   591	| arm | write spacing | runs | duplicates / 60 |
   592	|---|---|---|---|
   593	| tight | 1 ms | 10 | 31–33, mean **32.5** (**54.2 %**) |
   594	| spaced | 25 ms | 10 | **0** in all 10 |
   595	
   596	#### 5.5.1 MEASURED — two results, and they are the deliverable
   597	
   598	1. **The duplicate rate is CADENCE-SENSITIVE under this 1 ms embed / 1 ms write shape.** 32.5/60
   599	   at 1 ms spacing; **exactly 0/60 at 25 ms**, 10/10; and **0/96** for the §5.4 control, one
   600	   batch with no concurrent writer. So the ~50 % is a property of the write **cadence**, not a
   601	   constant of the scheduler, and **quoting "~50 %" without the cadence it was measured at is
   602	   not meaningful.**
   603	2. **An externally held WAL write lock makes discarded worker commits INVISIBLE.** §5.4: 86.4/96
   604	   duplicate embeds while `'failed'` terminals and `projection_failures` rows both read **zero**,
   605	   in all 10 runs.
   606	
   607	#### 5.5.2 HYPOTHESIS — strongly implicated, NOT confirmed
   608	
   609	The tempting third claim is *"TC-91 (a) **is** TC-91 (b)"* — that a duplicate **requires** a
   610	second writer holding the WAL write lock across the worker's commit, and that the engine's own
   611	writer is that holder in the baseline arms. An earlier draft of this document stated it flatly.
   612	**It is not proven, and it must be carried forward as a hypothesis with its gap named** (codex
   613	§9 round 4).
   614	
   615	The reason it cannot be closed here is worth stating precisely, because it is not an oversight
   616	in the harness — it is a property of the defect:
   617	
   618	> **There is NO direct instrumentation of worker commit failures in the baseline duplicate arms,
   619	> because the defect under study is precisely that those failures are discarded** by
   620	> `let _ = commit_projection_outcomes(..)` (`lib.rs:12711`, `lib.rs:12824`). Every counter that
   621	> could confirm the mechanism at baseline is the counter §5.4 proves is structurally blind.
   622	> **TC-91 (b) is the reason TC-91 (a) cannot be closed by measurement.**
   623	
   624	So what the two results above jointly support is: *removing writer/worker overlap removes the
   625	duplicates, and forcing that overlap externally reproduces them at ~90 % while the failure stays
   626	invisible.* What they do **not** support is that this is the only route to a duplicate, or that
   627	every one of the ~104/200 baseline duplicates arose this way. The forced-lock arm supplies the
   628	lock holder **externally**; it demonstrates the mechanism and the observability blind spot, but
   629	it cannot attribute the baseline population.
   630	
   631	The supporting code reading is real and points the same way — `commit_batch` holds
   632	`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` (`lib.rs:18637`, the TC-57 fix) for the duration of every write, while
   633	`commit_projection_outcomes` (`lib.rs:13809`) is a DEFERRED read-then-upgrade, so an overlap
   634	refuses the worker's promotion with plain `SQLITE_BUSY` and `let _ =` throws the outcome away.
   635	But it is a **reading**, and §0 of the TC-57 characterization is the standing warning about
   636	confident readings on this exact code path: every prior write-up named 517, and a fix scoped
   637	against it would have shipped inert. **Do not scope a 0.8.21 fix against an identity claim this
   638	slice did not establish.**
   639	
   640	#### 5.5.3 What would close it
   641	
   642	**R-C** (§7 — stop discarding the worker commit result) converts the hypothesis into a
   643	measurement: with a counter on the discarded commit, the baseline duplicate arms would show
   644	directly whether each duplicate was preceded by a refused promotion, and no forced-lock proxy
   645	would be needed. That is the principal reason R-C is recommended to land **first**.
   646	
   647	#### 5.5.4 What this does say about TC-57 §7 uncertainty 3
   648	
   649	TC-57 §8.2 measured worker-side pressure *relatively* — before vs after the caller-side fix —
   650	found no increase, and correctly declined to act. What it could not see, having no
   651	no-concurrency baseline to compare against, is that the ~104/200 it recorded as "pre-existing"
   652	was **already a defect** rather than a floor. §5.3's absolute baseline plus §5.5.1's zero at
   653	25 ms spacing supplies that comparison. This narrows TC-57's uncertainty 3 rather than closing
   654	it, on the same evidentiary limit as §5.5.2.
   655	
   656	### 5.6 Acceptance bar for the TC-91 fix at 0.8.21
   657	
   658	1. Remove the `#[ignore]` attributes from the TC-91 measurement arms as a RED→GREEN step.
   659	2. `tc91_duplicate_embed_rate_governed` and `_anonymous`: **0 duplicates / 200 rows** (were
   660	   ~104 and ~105).
   661	3. `tc91_mechanism_duplicate_rate_versus_write_cadence`: **tight arm 0**, spaced arm 0
   662	   (unchanged). Because the fix must remove the *cadence sensitivity*, not merely shift it, the
   663	   tight arm is the load-bearing assertion here.
   664	4. `tc91_control_unblocked_worker_commits`: unchanged at 0 duplicates.
   665	5. **`tc91_forced_commit_failure_is_invisible_to_terminal_counting` must be RE-READ, NOT
   666	   RE-RUN.** Its assertions encode the *defect*: they require `failed_terminals == 0` while
   667	   duplicates are non-zero. A fix that surfaces lost commits will make it fail, and that failure
   668	   is the **success signal**. Whoever lands the fix must rewrite this test to assert the new
   669	   contract (a lost commit is observable) and must say so explicitly in the commit message. It
   670	   is deliberately worded that way in the test's own failure text so this cannot be missed.
   671	6. `tc91_probe_embed_cost_per_open_at_head` stays green and unmodified, including its exact
   672	   `0 / 90 / 0` pins. It runs in the default profile, so a regression surfaces without `--ignored`.
   673	7. Non-vacuity must survive: `ingest_distinct == rows` and `vector_rows == rows` in every arm.
   674	   A "fix" that reduces duplicates by dropping rows is a regression, and these assertions are
   675	   what catch it. `probe_calls == 0` likewise — a duplicate count contaminated by probe embeds is
   676	   not a duplicate count.
   677	8. **Whoever lands this must resolve §5.5.2's hypothesis rather than inherit it.** If R-C lands
   678	   first (as §7 recommends), the instrumentation exists to say directly whether each baseline
   679	   duplicate followed a refused promotion — so *report that*, and correct §5.5 in either
   680	   direction. Do not carry "(a) is (b)" forward as though this slice had established it.
   681	
   682	---
