   242	### 2.4 The negative that was nearly filed — read this before trusting any 0/10
   243	
   244	The paced arm is the direct analogue of TC-57's repro: one writer, one row per write, 3 ms
   245	pacing, 2 ms embed. It is **0/10**, 1200 transitions, with a live worker and ~175 embeds per run.
   246	
   247	It does not reproduce because of arithmetic, not luck. The worker cannot hold the write lock
   248	until *at least* one embed latency after the competing write released the connection mutex,
   249	whereas `transition` travels from acquiring that same mutex to its promoting `UPDATE` in
   250	microseconds. At a 2 ms embed the worker is simply never there yet.
   251	
   252	Setting the embed delay to **0 ms** — plus a burst larger than `PROJECTION_COMMIT_BATCH` (16),
   253	so the worker's commit transaction is as long as it ever gets, and two writer threads matching
   254	`PROJECTION_WORKERS` — collapses that offset and the defect appears **10/10**.
   255	
   256	> **The general lesson, for the next agent to characterize a concurrency defect here: a 0/10 at
   257	> one load shape is not evidence of absence.** Name the parameter that governs the window and
   258	> vary *that*, or do not report a negative.
