   260	### 2.5 A second, distinct finding: `transition` becomes UNAVAILABLE under saturating load
   261	
   262	**MEASURED — `Engine::transition` only.** The first stress attempt used `writer_pace_ms: 0`
   263	(fully saturating, 2 threads × 24-row bursts). In **181 seconds** the loop issued **5**
   264	transitions, of which **4 failed with `EngineError::Scheduler`** — each having burned the full
   265	`LIFECYCLE_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS` (30 s) because `transition`'s own `drain` never reached idle.
   266	596,928 competing rows landed in that window.
   267	
   268	This is **not** the promote race. It is a separate availability property of the
   269	drain-then-act design: under sustained write pressure a lifecycle verb can be starved
   270	indefinitely, one 30-second timeout at a time, and the caller sees an opaque
