4. [src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/tc90_transition_write_race.rs:995](/home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/slice-23-sv/src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/tc90_transition_write_race.rs:995) makes live pin 3 timing-sensitive for the default merge gate. The test starts the blocker thread, sleeps 100 ms, then assumes the blocker has acquired the WAL write lock. Under loaded scheduling, the writer can run before the blocker actually holds the lock, making `elapsed >= HOLD / 2` fail even though the engine behavior is fine. The design doc’s “lock is HELD, not raced” framing at [dev/design/0.8.20-tc90-tc91-characterization.md:380](/home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/slice-23-sv/dev/design/0.8.20-tc90-tc91-characterization.md:380) is not quite true for pin 3’s setup. Use a readiness signal after `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` instead of a fixed sleep, or ignore-gate this pin.

**Closed**

F1 is closed in code: `assert_protocol_ran` pins `attempted == expected`, `truncated == false`, `competing_writes > 0`, `scheduler_errors == 0`, and `other_errors == 0`, and all four TC-90 loop arms call it. `ArmConfig::budget` can no longer silently truncate; a budget hit fails through both `attempted` and `truncated`. Applying it to the paced arms is not over-constraining; those arms are documented as full protocol runs with competing writes and no scheduler/other errors.

F2 is closed. The checked-in fixture has 45 non-empty, non-comment probe lines; `vector_equivalence_probes()` parses exactly that class of line; `probe_populate_or_check()` does populate then immediate check on an empty probe table. The live test pins `open1 == 0`, `open2 == PROBE_FIXTURE_SIZE * 2 == 90`, `open3 == 0`, so a regression to 45 would now fail.

F3 is partly closed: 13 tests / 9 ignored / 4 live is documented and matches the attributes. `tc91_probe_embed_cost_per_open_at_head` is deterministic enough for a live gate: no race, no wall-clock assertion, exact fixture-derived counts. Pin 3 is the exception noted above.

F4 is mostly closed in the design doc body: §5.5.1/§5.5.2 correctly split measured cadence/blindness from the unproven identity, and §5.6.8 forbids carrying “(a) is (b)” forward. §2.5’s presumed-not-measured split for sibling drain-first verbs is honest. Residual overclaim remains in the TC-91 test module header/assertion text and one R-A remedy sentence at [dev/design/0.8.20-tc90-tc91-characterization.md:727](/home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/slice-23-sv/dev/design/0.8.20-tc90-tc91-characterization.md:727), which says overlap is “exactly what drives” the duplicate rate.

